## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical data quality controls, schema evolution discipline, and safe deployment practices in multi-tenant streaming environments.

## Key Concepts
- Schema Registry with AVRO and compatibility enforcement
- Per-tenant schema namespaces and semantic versioning
- Real-time drift detection in Flink processing pipeline
- Automated quarantine and alerting mechanisms
- Circuit breaker patterns for data quality protection

## Code Example
```java
// Flink schema validation with drift detection
public class SchemaValidator extends ProcessFunction<GenericRecord, GenericRecord> {
    private final SchemaRegistryClient registry;

    @Override
    public void processElement(GenericRecord record, Context ctx) {
        try {
            Schema schema = registry.getLatestSchema(tenantTopic);
            if (!isValidAgainstSchema(record, schema)) {
                ctx.output(quarantineTag, record);
                alertDashboard("SCHEMA_DRIFT", tenantTopic);
            } else {
                ctx.output(validTag, record);
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            ctx.output(errorTag, record);
        }
    }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle non-breaking field removals while maintaining data lake query compatibility?
- How would you scale drift checks for high-throughput topics (>100K msg/sec)?
- What's your approach for schema evolution across multiple downstream consumers?

Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical debugging of startup-time issues caused by a sidecar container, including readiness ordering and configuration mounting verification, plus safe rollback procedures while maintaining latency targets. It emphasizes concrete kubectl usage and observable outcomes in a real-world production scenario.

Key Concepts
- Kubernetes rollout status and readiness probe verification
- Sidecar container impact on startup sequencing and latency
- ConfigMap and Secret mounting validation techniques
- Zero-downtime rollback strategies with latency constraints
- Production debugging workflow under load conditions

Code Example
```javascript
// Example: Node.js snippet for monitoring deployment status
const k8s = require('@kubernetes/client-node');
const kc = new k8s.KubeConfig();
kc.loadFromDefault();

const appsV1 = kc.makeApiClient(k8s.AppsV1Api);
const watch = new k8s.Watch(kc);

async function monitorRollout(namespace, deployment) {
  const stream = await watch.watch(`/apis/apps/v1/namespaces/${namespace}/deployments/${deployment}`,
    {}, // watch options
    (type, obj) => {
      if (obj.status.readyReplicas === obj.spec.replicas) {
        console.log('Rollout completed successfully');
      }
    });
}
```
